WEEK 4 REPORT
Predictive Modeling and Optimization in Logistics Systems
Project: Delivery Time Prediction for Logistics
Predictive modelling using the Amazon Delivery Dataset
Report detail	Description
Dataset	Amazon Delivery Dataset (Kaggle)
Original records	43,739
Target variable	Delivery_Time (minutes)
Models	Linear Regression and Random Forest
Training records	34,991
Testing records	8,748
Best-performing model	Random Forest Regressor
Best test result	MAE: 17.33 min; RMSE: 22.65 min; R²: 0.807

1. Introduction and Problem Definition
Delivery time is an important measure of logistics performance. It can vary according to distance, traffic, weather, vehicle type, delivery area and other operational factors. Estimating delivery duration can help logistics teams plan deliveries and identify orders that may need additional attention.
The problem addressed in this project is to predict the time required to complete a delivery using the information available in the Amazon Delivery Dataset. Since the target variable, Delivery_Time, is a numerical value measured in minutes, this is a supervised regression problem.
The objectives are to:
⦁	Prepare the cleaned delivery dataset for machine learning.
⦁	Train Linear Regression and Random Forest models.
⦁	Compare model performance using MAE, RMSE and R².
⦁	Validate the selected model using cross-validation.
⦁	Identify features that contribute to model predictions.
⦁	Suggest practical logistics improvements based on the analysis.
2. Dataset and Features
The project uses the Amazon Delivery Dataset from Kaggle. It contains 43,739 delivery records and information about delivery agents, locations, order timings, environmental conditions and delivery details.
Feature	Type	Description
Distance_km	Numerical	Calculated straight-line distance between store and delivery location
Agent_Age	Numerical	Age of the delivery agent
Agent_Rating	Numerical	Recorded delivery-agent rating
Order_Hour	Numerical	Hour at which the order was placed
Weather	Categorical	Weather condition during delivery
Traffic	Categorical	Traffic condition
Vehicle	Categorical	Vehicle used for delivery
Area	Categorical	Delivery area classification
Category	Categorical	Product category
Delivery_Time	Target	Actual delivery duration in minutes

Delivery_Time was used as the target variable, while the remaining selected columns were used as input features.
3. Data Preparation
The cleaned dataset prepared during Week 2 was used for model development. The target variable was separated from the input features, and the records were divided into training and testing sets using an 80:20 split.
⦁	Training data: 34,991 records.
⦁	Testing data: 8,748 records.
Missing numerical values were handled using median imputation. Missing categorical values were handled using the most frequent value. Categorical features were converted into numerical form using One-Hot Encoding.
A Scikit-learn pipeline was used to combine preprocessing and model training. The preprocessing steps were fitted on the training data only and then applied to the test data. This helps prevent information leakage from the test set.
Preprocessing code:
from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer
from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder

numeric_transformer = Pipeline(steps=[
    ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="median"))
])

categorical_transformer = Pipeline(steps=[
    ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="most_frequent")),
    ("encoder", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore"))
])

preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(transformers=[
    ("num", numeric_transformer, numeric_features),
    ("cat", categorical_transformer, categorical_features)
])
This approach ensures that the same preprocessing operations are applied consistently during training and prediction.
4. Model Selection And Reasoning
5.1 Linear Regression – Why was it selected?
Linear Regression was selected as a baseline model because it is simple, easy to understand, and computationally efficient. It helps identify basic relationships between input features and delivery time. Its performance also provides a reference point for comparing more advanced models.

5.2 Random Forest Regressor – Why was it selected?
Random Forest Regressor was selected because delivery time can be influenced by several factors that may have complex and non-linear relationships. It combines multiple decision trees and can capture patterns and interactions between features such as distance, traffic, and weather. It also provides feature-importance values that help identify influential input features.

5.3 Reason for Comparing Both Models
Both models were selected to compare a simple linear approach with a more flexible machine learning approach. This comparison helps determine whether a model capable of capturing complex patterns can improve delivery-time predictions..
5. Implementation
Python, Pandas and Scikit-learn were used for model development. The implementation extracts below follow the model-development steps and code patterns supplied for Week 4.
5.1 Training the models
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor
from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline

linear_model = Pipeline(steps=[
    ("preprocessor", preprocessor),
    ("model", LinearRegression())
])

rf_model = Pipeline(steps=[
    ("preprocessor", preprocessor),
    ("model", RandomForestRegressor(
        n_estimators=100,
        random_state=42,
        n_jobs=-1
    ))
])

linear_model.fit(X_train, y_train)
rf_model.fit(X_train, y_train)
Both models were trained using the same training data and preprocessing pipeline to make their performance comparable.
5.2 Model evaluation
from sklearn.metrics import (
    mean_absolute_error,
    mean_squared_error,
    r2_score
)
import numpy as np

predictions = rf_model.predict(X_test)
mae = mean_absolute_error(y_test, predictions)
rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions))
r2 = r2_score(y_test, predictions)

print("MAE:", mae)
print("RMSE:", rmse)
print("R2 Score:", r2)
The evaluation metrics measure prediction error and how well the model explains variation in delivery time.
5.3 Cross-validation
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score

scores = cross_val_score(
    rf_model,
    X_train,
    y_train,
    cv=3,
    scoring="neg_mean_absolute_error",
    n_jobs=1
)

print("Cross-validation MAE:", -scores)
print("Average MAE:", -scores.mean())
Three-fold cross-validation was used to check whether the Random Forest model performed consistently across different training and validation subsets.
5.4 Feature importance
feature_names = (
    rf_model.named_steps["preprocessor"]
    .get_feature_names_out()
)

importances = (
    rf_model.named_steps["model"]
    .feature_importances_
)

importance_df = pd.DataFrame({
    "Feature": feature_names,
    "Importance": importances
})

importance_df = importance_df.sort_values(
    by="Importance",
    ascending=False
)

print(importance_df.head(15))
Feature importance was extracted from the trained Random Forest model to identify which encoded input features contributed most to its predictions.
6. Evaluation and Validation
6.1 Evaluation metrics
Metric	Meaning
MAE	Measures the average absolute difference between actual and predicted delivery times. Lower values indicate better predictions.
RMSE	Measures prediction errors while giving greater importance to larger errors. Lower values indicate better performance.
R²	Measures how much variation in delivery time is explained by the model. A higher value indicates a better fit.

6.2 Model comparison results
Model	MAE (minutes)	RMSE (minutes)	R² Score
Linear Regression	25.34	32.02	0.615
Random Forest	17.33	22.65	0.807

Random Forest performed better than Linear Regression across all three metrics. Its MAE of 17.33 minutes indicates that its predictions differed from actual delivery times by an average absolute amount of approximately 17.33 minutes on the test set.
The R² score of 0.807 indicates that the model explained approximately 80.7% of the variation in delivery time in the test data. This is not the same as 80.7% prediction accuracy.
Based on these results, Random Forest was selected as the better-performing model for this project.
6.3 Cross-validation results
Fold	MAE (minutes)
Fold 1	17.83
Fold 2	17.55
Fold 3	17.80
Average	17.73

The average cross-validation MAE was 17.73 minutes. The three scores were close to one another, suggesting reasonably consistent performance across the folds.
No hyperparameter search was performed. The Random Forest model used 100 trees as a practical starting configuration for this project. Further tuning can be carried out in future work.
