7.3 Key observations
⦁	Grocery was the highest-ranked individual encoded feature in the reported importance output.
⦁	Agent rating and distance also had relatively high feature-importance values.
⦁	Traffic and weather categories contributed to the model’s predictions.
⦁	These values represent the model’s use of individual encoded features. They do not establish causation or show whether a feature increases or decreases delivery time.
8. Optimization Strategies
The following recommendations are proposals based on the dataset analysis and model results. They were not tested as live operational changes.
Strategy	Model/data insight it comes from	Recommended action	Expected benefit	Limitations
Traffic-aware planning	Traffic categories were included in the model and showed different delivery-time patterns in the earlier analysis	Consider traffic conditions when estimating delivery duration and planning dispatch	More realistic delivery-time estimates and better planning	Historical traffic categories may not represent current road conditions
Weather-aware planning	Weather categories contributed to predictions	Consider weather conditions when preparing delivery schedules and ETAs	Better preparation for potentially difficult delivery conditions	Weather effects were not tested through controlled experiments
Resource allocation by volume	Earlier analysis showed different numbers of delivery records across areas	Review delivery demand by area when planning delivery capacity	Better alignment between resources and recorded demand	Record counts do not directly measure staffing requirements
Grocery/category investigation	Grocery had the highest individual encoded feature importance	Examine Grocery deliveries further to understand their operational patterns	May reveal category-specific planning needs	Importance does not prove that Grocery orders take longer
High-ETA order review	The model can estimate delivery duration for individual records	Flag unusually high predicted delivery times for human review	Helps identify orders that may need additional attention	Predictions are estimates, not confirmed delays
Agent and operational factors	Agent age and rating appeared among important features	Review operational processes and provide appropriate support or training where needed	May help identify areas for operational improvement	Do not use these features to rank or penalise individual agents; importance does not establish individual performance or causation

9. Limitations and Future Work
9.1 Limitations
1.	Dataset coverage: The model was trained using one historical dataset, so its performance may differ on new delivery data.
2.	Missing operational information: The dataset does not contain promised delivery times, live GPS routes, actual road conditions or transportation costs. Therefore, official delay rates, on-time rates and cost savings cannot be calculated.
3.	Geographic data quality: Suspicious distance values were identified during preprocessing and treated as missing. The calculated distance is a straight-line estimate, not an actual road route.
4.	Model tuning: Extensive hyperparameter tuning was not performed, and only two models were compared in the initial implementation.
5.	Interpretation: Feature importance indicates how the model used the inputs; it does not prove cause and effect.
6.	Validation approach: A random train-test split was used. A time-based split would provide another way to evaluate performance on later deliveries.
9.2 Future Work
The project can be improved in the following ways:
⦁	Applying RandomizedSearchCV or GridSearchCV to tune Random Forest parameters.
⦁	Comparing Random Forest with Decision Tree and Gradient Boosting models.
⦁	Testing a time-based train-test split.
⦁	Integrating live traffic and GPS route information, if available.
⦁	Collecting transportation cost data for a separate cost-analysis study.
10. Conclusion
This project developed and compared Linear Regression and Random Forest models to predict delivery time using the Amazon Delivery Dataset. Random Forest performed better, achieving an MAE of 17.33 minutes, RMSE of 22.65 minutes and R² score of 0.807 on the test data. Cross-validation showed reasonably consistent results, while feature-importance analysis highlighted several relevant delivery-related inputs. The findings provide a basis for delivery-time estimation and practical logistics planning recommendations, although further testing and additional operational data would be needed before real-world deployment.
